NOT ORIGINAL WORK

  driver/xc2028.h
    A partial verbatim copy of drivers/media/tuners/xc2028.h from Linux v6.16,
    Copyright (c) 2007-2008 Mauro Carvalho Chehab <mchehab@kernel.org>,
    GPL-2.0, trimmed to the declarations this driver uses.

    It is copied rather than included because that header is private: it lives
    under drivers/ instead of include/media/, so it ships in no kernel-devel
    package and an out-of-tree module cannot reach it. The file's own comment
    explains the coupling risk this creates and how to refresh it.

  Structure, not code
    driver/gb-alsa.c follows the shape of the in-tree usbtv-audio.c, and the
    URB-completion frame assembler follows usbtv and stk1160. Those drivers were
    read for structure; no code was copied from them. Where the resemblance is
    close enough to matter, the source files say so in comments.

  Register knowledge
    The CX25840 initialisation sequences come from the chip's datasheet, from
    the in-tree cx25840 driver's behaviour, and from observing Adaptec's Windows
    driver on the wire. docs/internals/ records which facts came from which.


DELIBERATELY ABSENT: THE FIRMWARE

The device cannot work without Adaptec's 8051 firmware, and it is not in this
repository -- neither the binary nor any image extracted from it. The
firmware is fetched before building from a public mirror of the original
driver CD and extracted locally, on the machine that will use it, by tools in this
repository.

This is deliberate, not an oversight, and the short version is: 
extracting firmware from a driver you legitimately possess, to operate hardware
you own, is interoperability. Redistributing that firmware is a separate act,
a different legal question, and this project gets no benefit from taking it on.
Adaptec was acquired in 2010 and the product line is long dead, so there is 
nobody left to ask for a grant.

About two dozen lines of annotated 8051 disassembly are quoted in
docs/internals/ (README.md, ir-receiver.md and tuner.md), each next to the
finding it supports. They are short excerpts, not the firmware: nothing can be
rebuilt from them, but were needed by a reader to check those findings.


